      fitted exponent p in err ~ R^-p, on disjoint pairs:
        R = 2 -> 4:  p = 1.861
        R = 3 -> 6:  p = 2.550
        R = 4 -> 8:  p = 4.850
        spread 2.989 -- consistent power law: False

      THE CORRECTION IS NOT SHORT-RANGED, and the docstring of
      _bloch_contact_correction said it was.  That claim is now
      retracted there.  <G> - G ~ d^2/r^3 and a 2-D shell at radius R
      holds ~8R cells, so each shell gives ~d^2/R^2 and the TAIL
      beyond R goes like d^2/R.  A 1/R tail cannot be summed away by
      enlarging the box: va_all_reach MEASURES the truncation, it
      does not remove it.  Removing it needs an analytic tail.

      ⚠ UNCHECKED RISK: a 1/R tail in a 2-D lattice sum is exactly
      the regime where the limit can depend on the summation SHAPE,
      the same structure already found in the k=0 lattice sum of the
      point propagator.  Any reach-extrapolated number is provisional
      until that is settled.

==============================================================================
WHY NOT COLLOCATION FOR EVERYTHING -- the structural half

  Collocation constrains the TEST space (one equation per cell at its
  centre).  The SOURCE side is always an integral over the cell,
  because at the self cell the point value of dd-G does not exist --
  a non-integrable 1/r^3 plus the Eshelby delta -- and at contact the
  cells touch.  So the scheme cannot be 'collocation everywhere' even
  in principle; the self integral IS the T-matrix.

  The real question is where the MIDPOINT RULE is an adequate
  approximation to that source integral, and that is what panel [1]
  and [3] measure.  Answer: NOT at the shells it is currently used
  on.  Correcting them at the DEFAULT reach is worth ~9x on the
  Kennett residual, and the gain grows under refinement -- a
  scale-invariant bias removed, not a discretisation error reduced.

  ⚠ BUT ~9x IS NOT THE ANSWER, IT IS THE ANSWER AT reach = 4.
  Panel [4] shows that default is itself a truncation worth a further
  factor ~3 by reach 8, with no saturation in sight.  The converged
  value is UNKNOWN and cannot be reached by raising the parameter:
  the tail is O(1/R).  Quote the ~9x as 'at the current default',
  never as the size of the effect.

  ⚠ ALSO NOT ESTABLISHED.  The refinement sequence in panel [1] is
  still falling but NOT at a clean second-order rate (1.20, 1.16 per
  halving), so it is not yet a convergence claim -- and part of that
  is the shell truncation panel [4] measures, not physics.
